<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Embedded into Pysar.dll as ILLink.Descriptors.xml, which the trimmer reads out of every assembly
  it processes - no consumer opt-in, and nothing for a head to remember.

  Report markup names types and properties as strings, and nothing in the compiled code points at
  them: XamlTypeResolver resolves an element through asm.GetType($"{clr}.{localName}"),
  XamlMemberAssigner assigns through GetProperty(...).SetValue(...), and ValueConverter turns an
  attribute into an enum through Enum.Parse. The trimmer sees none of it. Published trimmed and
  without this file, Pysar.Elements.Text keeps its type and loses all eleven of its setters, and
  the first report to set Content fails at InitializeComponent.

  The preserved set is the element model and everything markup reaches through it: the namespaces
  AssemblyInfo.cs declares through XmlnsDefinition, the base namespace those elements inherit from,
  and the structs and enums their properties are typed as. Elements.Base is not optional and not
  obvious - a namespace here matches exactly, not by prefix, and markup sets Width, Margin and
  HorizontalAlignment far more often than anything declared on the leaf type. Leave it out and the
  failure moves rather than disappears, from "type not found" to ArgumentException/
  Arg_SetMethNotFnd out of PropertyInfo.SetValue.

  Listing a namespace with no children preserves every type and member under it; the layers markup
  cannot reach (Binding, Export, Skia) stay trimmable, and TrimmerRootsTests holds both halves.

  This covers Pysar's own assembly only. A consumer's converters, custom elements and the data
  models behind {Binding} are resolved the same reflective way out of the consumer's assembly -
  Pysar.Xaml.targets roots that one.
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="Pysar">
    <namespace fullname="Pysar.Elements" />
    <namespace fullname="Pysar.Elements.Base" />
    <namespace fullname="Pysar.Core.Structs" />
    <namespace fullname="Pysar.Core.Enums" />
  </assembly>
</linker>
